// The syntax tree, and GAP 1's real evidence.

export enum Operator {
	ADD,
	SUBTRACT,
	MULTIPLY,
	EQUALS,
}

Operator implements Copy

// FINDING 1 — this is the recursive value type `examples/QuadTree` was supposed
// to be evidence for, and was not.
//
// QuadTree's GAP 1 says a recursive value type has no size, and
// `tree-places.elp` FINDING 1 found that QuadTree's own enum does not actually
// demonstrate it: both variants hold an array, an array must be a heap buffer
// to grow, so the indirection is already there and the type lays out fine.
//
// `Expr` has no such escape. `BINARY` holds two `Expr` **directly**, so the
// size equation is `size(Expr) = tag + 2 * size(Expr)`, which has no finite
// solution. `LET` and `IF` are the same shape. There is no array anywhere to
// supply the indirection, and an AST is the single most common recursive type
// any language compiles.
//
// `memory-model.md` has `Copy`, `Move`, `Protected` and `Binding<T>`, and not
// one of them says "boxed". Rust writes `Box<Expr>`, Swift sidesteps it by
// making the recursive case a `class`, C++ uses a pointer, and OCaml and
// Haskell box everything so the question never arises.
//
// RESOLVED — `indirect` is ratified in `memory-model.md`, and this is the
// migrated form. The marker is on the *field*, not on the case: an enum case's
// payload entries are fields, so one rule covers `enum` and `object` alike,
// at the cost of one allocation per marked field where Swift's case-level
// marker would make one per case.
//
// The box is immutable, which is the load-bearing half. It leaves `Expr`
// `Copy` — a pointer copy and a retain — and that is what dissolves FINDING 3
// over in `runtime.elp`.
export enum Expr {
	LITERAL { of int },
	NAME { of string },

	BINARY { op Operator, indirect left Expr, indirect right Expr },

	// `LET` binds and does not permit the bound name inside its own value.
	// `LETREC` does. Keeping them separate is what makes FINDING 4 provable
	// rather than a matter of how the evaluator happens to be written.
	LET { name string, indirect value Expr, indirect body Expr },
	LETREC { name string, indirect value Expr, indirect body Expr },

	LAMBDA { parameter string, indirect body Expr },
	CALL { indirect callee Expr, indirect argument Expr },

	IF {
		indirect condition Expr,
		indirect then Expr,
		indirect otherwise Expr,
	},
}

// FINDING 10 — NEW, and produced by the migration.
//
// Every case but two is entirely indirect, which is eight allocations for a
// three-node expression. Swift's case-level marker would make three. The
// field-level rule is still right — it is one rule rather than two — but this
// file is the evidence for the sugar `memory-model.md` parks as additive, and
// a compiler that coalesces a case's indirect fields into one allocation would
// be invisible under rule 1 and is the better answer than new syntax.

// vim: ft=kotlin
